course: religion-ethics
lesson: 16
title: The miracles of Jesus
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Gospels tell of amazing things Jesus did: turning water into wine, feeding a huge crowd, calming a storm, healing the sick and even raising his friend Lazarus from death.
explain: Christians call these miracles, and John's Gospel calls them «signs», because they point beyond themselves to who Jesus is and to God's care for people.
explain: Many miracles happen to ordinary people in ordinary trouble: a wedding running out of wine, a hungry crowd, a frightened crew, a blind beggar shouting by the road.
explain: Some show what people around Jesus did too: a boy shared his lunch, four friends carried a man to Jesus, and one healed man came back to say thank you.
explain: For a child today the stories say: bring what you have, help your friends reach help, keep asking when others tell you to be quiet, and remember to say thank you.
explain: Whether miracles really happen is something people disagree about; Christians believe God can act in the world, and the olympiad way is to talk about it with reasons and respect.
fact: A miracle is an amazing event that Christians believe shows God's power at work beyond the ordinary.
fact: John's Gospel calls Jesus' miracles «signs», because they point to who Jesus is.
fact: At a wedding in Cana Jesus turned water into wine when the wine ran out; John calls it his first sign.
fact: Jesus fed five thousand people with a boy's five loaves and two fish, and twelve baskets were left over.
fact: In a storm on the lake Jesus said to the wind and waves, «Peace, be still», and they became calm.
fact: Peter walked on the water towards Jesus but began to sink when he looked at the waves; Jesus caught him.
fact: Blind Bartimaeus kept shouting for Jesus even when people told him to be quiet, and Jesus healed him.
fact: Four friends lowered a paralysed man through a roof to Jesus, who forgave him and healed him.
fact: Jesus healed ten men with leprosy, but only one, a Samaritan, came back to say thank you.
fact: At the tomb of his friend Lazarus «Jesus wept», and then he called Lazarus back to life.
fact: Christians treasure «Jesus wept» because it shows Jesus shares people's sadness when someone dies.
fact: Christians believe God can act in the world; sceptics doubt that miracles happen; some Christians read some miracle stories symbolically. This is disputed.
exercise: What do Christians mean by a miracle? | An amazing event showing God's power beyond the ordinary | Any lucky thing that happens by chance, like finding money | A clever magic trick done to impress | A story that only children believe
exercise: Why does John's Gospel call the miracles «signs»? | They point to who Jesus is and to God's care | They were written up on signs along the street | They were warnings of danger sent to the crowds | They showed travellers the right way to the Temple
exercise: Where did Jesus turn water into wine? | At a wedding in Cana | At the Last Supper | At a funeral in Bethany | At the Temple in Jerusalem
exercise: Why might Jesus' first sign have been at a party? | He cared about ordinary joy and celebration | He wanted to drink more of the good wine himself | Parties were the only place crowds met | He wanted to stop the wedding
exercise: What did the boy give for the feeding of the five thousand? | Five loaves and two fish | Two loaves and five fish | Twelve baskets of bread | Some fruit and water
exercise: How much food was left over after the five thousand had eaten? | Twelve baskets | Nothing at all | One small basket | Seven loaves
exercise: What did Jesus say to the wind and the waves in the storm? | «Peace, be still» | «Go and sin no more» | «Let there be light» | «Follow me»
exercise: What made Peter begin to sink when he walked on the water? | He looked at the waves and grew afraid | His boat tipped him over | Jesus let go of his hand halfway across the water | He was carrying too much
exercise: What did people tell blind Bartimaeus to do when he shouted for Jesus? | Be quiet | Go home and wash | Shout louder | Climb a tree to see
exercise: How did four friends get the paralysed man to Jesus? | They lowered him through the roof | They waited for the crowd to leave | They carried him to the lake | They asked Jesus to come outside
exercise: What did Jesus do for the paralysed man first? | He forgave him | He told him to go away | He gave him bread | He asked him to pay
exercise: How many of the ten healed lepers came back to say thank you? | One | Ten | Five | None
exercise: Who was the leper who came back to thank Jesus? | A Samaritan | A priest | A Roman soldier | One of the twelve disciples
exercise: What did Jesus do at the tomb of Lazarus before calling him out? | He wept | He laughed | He walked away | He scolded Martha
exercise: Why is «Jesus wept» an important verse for Christians? | It shows Jesus shares our grief | It is the longest verse in the Bible | It shows Jesus was angry with Lazarus | It proves Lazarus was not really dead
exercise: Which is true about whether miracles happen? | People disagree, and it can be discussed with reasons | Everyone agrees miracles happen every day | All Christians read every miracle the same way | Scientists have proved every miracle story
test: My little brother says Jesus fed the five thousand with twelve loaves and had five baskets left. What do you put right? | It was five loaves and two fish, with twelve baskets left over | It was two loaves and twelve fish, with five baskets left | It was five thousand loaves and no leftovers | It was twelve fish and five baskets of bread
test: «my mate says miracles are just magic tricks lol». What would a Christian say the miracles are meant to do? | Point to who Jesus is and to God's care | Make Jesus rich and famous | Scare people into obeying | Entertain the crowds at parties
test: A classmate who is not religious asks: «you seriously believe a man walked on water?» What is the best reply? | Christians believe God can act in the world, but I get why you doubt it | You're stupid if you don't believe it | Nobody has ever doubted it, so it must be true | It doesn't matter what anyone thinks about it
test: «ugh everyone keeps telling me to shut up when I ask for help in class». Which miracle story fits best? | Bartimaeus kept calling for Jesus when told to be quiet | The water turned into wine at Cana for the wedding guests | The twelve baskets of bread were collected | Lazarus was in the tomb four days
test: Your sister says only Jesus did anything in the feeding of the five thousand. What did someone else give? | A boy gave his five loaves and two fish | The disciples baked bread for everyone | The crowd brought twelve baskets of food | A rich man paid for the meal
test: Your friend's in hospital and can't come to the party. Which miracle shows friends helping someone reach help? | Four friends lowering a man through a roof | Peter walking on the water | The water turned into wine | The calming of the storm
test: «got loads of presents, can't be bothered with thank-you cards». Which story would a Sunday-school teacher tell? | Only one of ten healed lepers came back to thank Jesus | Jesus turned the water into wine at a wedding party in Cana | The boy shared his lunch | Lazarus was raised from the tomb
test: Mum says she's scared about the operation. Which story do Christians use for trusting Jesus in frightening times? | Jesus calming the storm | The ten lepers going to the priests | The wedding at Cana | The twelve baskets of leftovers
test: A friend says, «Peter sank because he couldn't swim.» What does the story actually say? | He sank when he looked at the waves and was afraid | He sank because the boat had drifted too far away from him | He sank because Jesus pushed him | He sank because it was night
test: A judge asks: «Can a Christian who doubts some miracle stories still be a Christian?» What is the fairest answer? | Yes, some Christians read some stories symbolically; this is disputed | No, every Christian must believe every story in the same way | Yes, because miracles do not matter to any Christian | No, because doubting is always a sin
test: Grandad died and your cousin asks if God cares when people are sad. Which verse do Christians often point to? | «Jesus wept» at the tomb of Lazarus | «Peace, be still» in the storm | «Let my people go» when Moses spoke to Pharaoh | «Love your enemies» on the hill
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance attributed to the miracles of Jesus in the Gospel according to John? | They are signs which point to the identity of Jesus | They are entertainments intended for the crowds | They are punishments inflicted upon unbelievers | They are ordinary events without further meaning
exam: Ascertain the quantity of provisions furnished by a boy for the feeding of five thousand persons. | Five loaves and two fish | Two loaves and five fish | Twelve loaves and seven fish | Five thousand loaves and fish
exam: Of ten persons healed of leprosy, state the number who subsequently returned to render thanks, together with his origin. | One, a Samaritan | Ten, all of them Jews | Two, both of them priests | None of them returned
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established positions concerning the possibility of miracles. | The question is disputed and may be discussed respectfully with reasons | It has been demonstrated by scientific means that miracles occur every day | It has been conclusively demonstrated that no miracle can occur | Every Christian interprets every miracle literally
